{"id":"neet-2026r-q004","pn":4,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Oscillations","st":"SHM velocity-displacement graph","q":"Consider a spring-mass simple harmonic oscillator in one dimension. The mass of the particle is m kg and the spring constant is k Nm⁻¹. At a given instant, the extension of the spring is x-meter and the speed of the particle is v ms⁻¹. On the x - v plane, if the graph of v as a function of x is a circle, then the correct option is:","o":["k = m","k = m²","k = √m","k = 1/m"],"c":0,"s":"Velocity in SHM: v = ω√(A² – x²).\nv² = ω²(A² – x²).\nv²/ω² + x² = A².\nThis is the equation of a circle, so ω² = 1.\nk/m = 1.\nSo k = m."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q025","pn":25,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Oscillations","st":"Energy in SHM","q":"For a simple pendulum, having time period T, the variation of kinetic energy (K.E.) with time (t) is represented by:","o":["K.E. oscillating as a sine-like wave about a dashed mean level above the axis, with marks at T/2 and T","K.E. as a sine wave starting at 0 going positive then negative below the axis, marks at T/2 and T","K.E. as repeated positive humps (always ≥ 0), each hump per half period, with marks at T/2 and T","K.E. a constant horizontal line, mark at T"],"c":2,"s":"Kinetic energy K = (1/2)mv².\nFor SHM, v = Aω cos(ωt + φ), so K = (1/2)mA²ω²cos²(ωt + φ).\nTherefore K ∝ cos²(ωt + φ).\nThis is never negative and repeats every T/2, so it appears as a series of positive humps (option 3).","fig":{"w":470,"h":300,"alt":"Four graphs (1)-(4) of kinetic energy K.E. against time t, with T/2 and T marked","d":[["l",60,125,60,25,"a"],["l",60,125,225,125,"a"],["t",57,43,"K.E.","es"],["t",50,138,"0","es"],["t",230,139,"t","s"],["t",12,80,"(1)","s"],["l",60,88,200,88,"d"],["p","M60 88 C82 50 106 50 128 88 C150 126 174 126 196 88"],["t",132,80,"T/2","s"],["t",196,76,"T","s"],["l",196,83,196,93],["l",290,125,290,25,"a"],["l",290,125,455,125,"a"],["t",287,43,"K.E.","es"],["t",280,138,"0","es"],["t",460,139,"t","s"],["t",242,80,"(2)","s"],["p","M290 125 C312 67 336 67 358 125 C380 157 404 157 426 125"],["l",358,121,358,129],["t",360,115,"T/2","s"],["l",426,121,426,129],["t",422,143,"T","s"],["l",60,275,60,175,"a"],["l",60,275,225,275,"a"],["t",57,193,"K.E.","es"],["t",50,288,"0","es"],["t",230,289,"t","s"],["t",12,230,"(3)","s"],["p","M65 275 Q94 211 123 275"],["p","M123 275 Q152 211 181 275"],["p","M181 275 Q192 239 204 255"],["l",123,271,123,279],["t",115,291,"T/2","s"],["l",192,271,192,279],["t",188,291,"T","s"],["l",290,275,290,175,"a"],["l",290,275,455,275,"a"],["t",287,193,"K.E.","es"],["t",280,288,"0","es"],["t",460,289,"t","s"],["t",242,230,"(4)","s"],["l",290,230,425,230],["l",422,271,422,279],["t",418,291,"T","s"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q034","pn":34,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Oscillations","st":"Floating cylinder oscillation period","q":"A cylindrical cork of uniform density floats in a liquid of density ρ₁. If the cork is depressed slightly and released, it oscillates harmonically with time period T. If the same cork floats in another liquid of density ρ₂, then the similar oscillation has time period 2T. The value of ρ₂ / ρ₁ is:","o":["2","1/2","1/4","4"],"c":2,"s":"Time period of a cylinder oscillating in a liquid: T = 2π√(ρₛl/(ρₗg)).\nThe density ρₛ of the cork is constant.\nSo T₁/T₂ = √(ρ₂/ρ₁).\nT/(2T) = √(ρ₂/ρ₁).\nρ₂/ρ₁ = 1/4."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q036","pn":36,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Oscillations","st":"Energy in SHM","q":"The sum of kinetic energy and potential energy of a simple pendulum bob is 0.02 joule. The speed of the simple pendulum bob at equilibrium position is approximately:\n(Consider mass of the bob = 20 g)","o":["1.41 m/s","14.1 m/s","0.2 m/s","2.0 m/s"],"c":0,"s":"At the equilibrium position the potential energy is zero, so total energy = kinetic energy.\n(1/2)mv² = 0.02.\n(1/2) × 20 × 10⁻³ × v² = 2 × 10⁻².\nv² = 2, so v = √2 = 1.41 m/s."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q023","pn":23,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Oscillations","st":"Spring-mass system, variable mass","q":"In an oscillating spring mass system, a spring is connected to a box filled with sand. As the box oscillates, sand leaks slowly out of the box vertically so that the average frequency ω(t) and average amplitude A(t) of the system change with time t. Which one of the following options schematically depicts these changes correctly?","o":["ω(t) rises with time and flattens at a higher value; A(t) stays constant (horizontal line)","ω(t) rises with time and flattens at a higher value; A(t) falls with time and flattens at a lower value","ω(t) rises with time and flattens at a higher value; A(t) rises with time and flattens at a higher value","ω(t) falls with time and flattens at a lower value; A(t) falls with time and flattens at a lower value"],"c":1,"s":"At any time the time period is T = 2π√(m/k).\nHere m is decreasing, so the time period T decreases.\nSince ω = 2π/T, as mass leaks, ω increases.\nNow, at any instant mg = kx₀.\nSo the equilibrium length x₀ = mg/k, where m is decreasing.\nSo the equilibrium length decreases.\nSo the amplitude also goes on decreasing.","fig":{"w":490,"h":207,"alt":"Four options, each a pair of graphs of omega(t) above and A(t) below against time t","d":[["l",69,48,69,4.8,"a"],["l",31.8,48,174,48,"a"],["t",43.2,28.2,"ω(t)","s"],["t",177.2,51.9,"t","s"],["p","M69 24 C84 10.8 108 7.8 135 7.8"],["l",69,94.8,69,52.2,"a"],["l",31.8,94.8,174,94.8,"a"],["t",43.2,75,"A(t)","s"],["t",177.2,98.7,"t","s"],["l",69,72,133.8,72],["l",369,48,369,4.8,"a"],["l",331.8,48,474,48,"a"],["t",343.2,28.2,"ω(t)","s"],["t",477.2,51.9,"t","s"],["p","M369 22.8 C384 9.6 408 6.6 433.2 6.6"],["l",369,94.8,369,52.2,"a"],["l",331.8,94.8,474,94.8,"a"],["t",343.2,75,"A(t)","s"],["t",477.2,98.7,"t","s"],["p","M376.2 57 C384 76.8 402 88.8 433.2 88.8"],["l",69,151.2,69,108,"a"],["l",31.8,151.2,174,151.2,"a"],["t",43.2,131.4,"ω(t)","s"],["t",177.2,155.1,"t","s"],["p","M69 127.2 C84 114 108 111 135 111"],["l",69,198,69,155.4,"a"],["l",31.8,198,174,198,"a"],["t",43.2,178.2,"A(t)","s"],["t",177.2,201.9,"t","s"],["p","M69 177 C84 163.2 108 160.2 135 160.2"],["l",369,151.2,369,108,"a"],["l",331.8,151.2,474,151.2,"a"],["t",343.2,131.4,"ω(t)","s"],["t",477.2,155.1,"t","s"],["p","M376.2 111 C384 132 402 145.8 433.2 145.8"],["l",369,198,369,155.4,"a"],["l",331.8,198,474,198,"a"],["t",343.2,178.2,"A(t)","s"],["t",477.2,201.9,"t","s"],["p","M376.2 160.8 C384 180 402 192 433.2 192"],["t",4.2,61.2,"(1)"],["t",304.2,61.2,"(2)"],["t",4.2,165,"(3)"],["t",304.2,165,"(4)"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2025-q044","pn":44,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Oscillations","st":"Spring-mass SHM amplitude","q":"Two identical point masses P and Q, suspended from two separate massless springs of spring constants k₁ and k₂, respectively, oscillate vertically. If their maximum speeds are the same, the ratio (A_Q/A_P) of the amplitude A_Q of mass Q to the amplitude A_P of mass P is","o":["k₂/k₁","k₁/k₂","√(k₂/k₁)","√(k₁/k₂)"],"c":3,"s":"Maximum velocity V = Aω.\nv_P = v_Q, so A_Pω_P = A_Qω_Q.\nA_Q/A_P = ω_P/ω_Q, with ω = √(k/m).\nA_Q/A_P = √(k_P m_Q/(m_P k_Q)).\nThe masses are identical, so A_Q/A_P = √(k₁/k₂)."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q031","pn":31,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Oscillations","st":"SHM equation","q":"If 5sin(πt + π/3) m represents the motion of a particle executing simple harmonic motion, the amplitude and time period of motion, respectively, are;","o":["5 cm, 1 s","5 m, 1 s","5 cm, 2 s","5 m, 2 s"],"c":3,"s":"x = 5 sin(πt + π/3) m.\nAmplitude = 5 m.\nω = π = 2π/T.\nT = 2π/π = 2 s."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q048","pn":48,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Oscillations","st":"Simple pendulum time period","q":"If the mass of the bob in a simple pendulum is increased to thrice its original mass and its length is made half its original length, then the new time period of oscillation is x/2 times its original time period. Then the value of x is:","o":["2√3","4","√3","√2"],"c":3,"s":"Time period T = 2π√(ℓ/g), which does not depend on the mass.\nNew length ℓ′ = ℓ/2, so T′ = 2π√(ℓ/2g).\nGiven T′ = (x/2) T, so 2π√(ℓ/2g) = (x/2) × 2π√(ℓ/g).\nThis gives 1/√2 = x/2.\nSo x = √2."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q038","pn":38,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Oscillations","st":"SHM from x-t graph","q":"The x-t graph of a particle performing simple harmonic motion is shown in the figure. The acceleration of the particle at t = 2 s is :","o":["–(π²/16) ms⁻²","(π²/8) ms⁻²","–(π²/8) ms⁻²","(π²/16) ms⁻²"],"c":0,"s":"From the graph, amplitude A = 1 m and the period is 8 s, so ω = 2π/8 = π/4.\nx = sin(πt/4).\nv = (π/4) cos(πt/4).\na = −(π²/16) sin(πt/4).\nAt t = 2 s, sin(π/2) = 1, so a = −π²/16 m/s².","fig":{"w":310,"h":150,"alt":"x–t graph of SHM: from the origin up to x = 1 at t = 2 s, zero at 4 s, minimum x = −1 at 6 s, zero at 8 s.","d":[["l",50,140,50,6,"a"],["l",20,75,300,75,"a"],["p","M50 75C64.7 51.4 78.2 30 94 30C109.8 30 123.3 51.4 138 75C152.7 98.6 166.2 120 182 120C197.8 120 211.3 98.6 226 75","B"],["l",50,30,94,30,"d"],["l",94,30,94,75,"d"],["l",50,120,182,120,"d"],["l",182,120,182,75,"d"],["t",44,34,"1","e"],["t",44,124,"–1","e"],["t",44,89,"0","e"],["t",94,89,"2","m"],["t",146,70,"4","m"],["t",182,67,"6","m"],["t",230,89,"8","m"],["t",10,63,"x (m)"],["t",296,67,"t (s)","e"]]},"sfig":{"w":250,"h":125,"alt":"Sketch of x = sin(ωt): amplitude 1, zero crossings at t = 4 and t = 8.","d":[["l",40,115,40,8,"a"],["l",40,65,230,65,"a"],["p","M40 65C53.3 46.7 65.6 30 80 30C94.4 30 106.7 46.7 120 65C133.3 83.3 145.6 100 160 100C174.4 100 186.7 83.3 200 65","B"],["l",40,30,80,30,"d"],["t",35,34,"1","e"],["t",126,59,"4","m"],["t",202,57,"8","m"],["t",48,12,"x"],["t",226,81,"t","m"]]}}
